#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e2ed19-98ac-5bcb-a221-0caee13960fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260093} {:answer/id #uuid "337fdb38-6a77-58a2-a8ba-0427000097ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260341} {:answer/id #uuid "bc81f5e4-801a-52a8-8bc4-b60b5b28c7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261536} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c1ff6-71eb-5c98-bf53-9f9db4823148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261667} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0dfe85-b143-550c-becd-1c76a74d08ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c1ff6-71eb-5c98-bf53-9f9db4823148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261667} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d96bed-7e56-5fe7-b897-4e96898d3e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4e547a4-3c18-52fb-b4b9-408da110d4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126820" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d24203b1-5e2d-543b-b875-84082292041c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168706} {:answer/id #uuid "12450fac-5904-57a0-92e2-bc8a3468008b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168707} {:answer/id #uuid "23c8d906-57d9-5dfc-98ed-afb30ba1cec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168708} {:answer/id #uuid "58920047-051c-5179-a311-88f110077114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168709} {:answer/id #uuid "5d55495b-d3c0-5a44-b16e-2735230596b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d24203b1-5e2d-543b-b875-84082292041c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168706} :answer-field/id #uuid "744dba16-b85d-5809-875d-1211f267487f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceef6c7e-683c-550f-97a1-9156380979d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126821" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/37/3786d0a5fb99d1a32d761e067f9c49b6b7da05a11fc555dd94a4ef42d753f129.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x-{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-1)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f014674-85e2-5712-94fa-a1a4f4088850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260429} {:answer/id #uuid "540fd8b7-839b-5252-8a65-5ad6720e3081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260583} {:answer/id #uuid "69707d0e-2e50-52a2-a276-f84bd14a62a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260796} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb13e75-d527-5698-b332-2b8a249bc796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261849} {:answer/id #uuid "fc605dab-f873-5fb2-8c9e-b56b75167414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb13e75-d527-5698-b332-2b8a249bc796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261849} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1824784-022c-5d45-b426-731053e70693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db5d90ba-c5db-5d34-b26f-640edea7636c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127303" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35e378bca35f4b0822c713556065415d59b5c566ec8fcc8e3124256aac7b7f14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{5}-3y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-(-1))\\,dy \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21de3cf7-4b55-5b4f-ab6a-375c5a7d25e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260167} {:answer/id #uuid "a2335fe5-8b47-5e4b-835b-e3b58b0f5ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261286} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1ad8f1-09f9-5de7-ba25-01fe8f576f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261507} {:answer/id #uuid "c26a51aa-0eb7-55e1-8247-693120014201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261590} {:answer/id #uuid "f5911c8b-0c20-5a8d-91de-79dbd76c58cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21de3cf7-4b55-5b4f-ab6a-375c5a7d25e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260167} :answer-field/id #uuid "80d4081e-14ed-54f2-ad21-1cd5742e88d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb71543-2fec-5ce1-b3ae-7f7da8898b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-127308" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3},\\,x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/2696cdadd3cb020cfbe343b9c372893ac18b63f3548ff71003b7328b19de39d5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-3{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(-3(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-(-3(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6)\\,dy \\\\  & =-6y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ef51b5-bef5-548c-8f51-a79f305af85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259957} {:answer/id #uuid "80081f79-d17b-5546-ab52-2540982bd374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260986} {:answer/id #uuid "806a70e6-fd99-58c6-a425-5c06d2336cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260991} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1969e2-dc11-53e4-a52a-96db98443bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc96c67-0441-581c-839f-b9e98cb25a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef1969e2-dc11-53e4-a52a-96db98443bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0261ce20-dc45-5b39-aeaf-012d29e230ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5702cf64-a04c-533d-bb19-d90253b29fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-127322" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2058c9-6db4-581f-a065-7bb1cf07c556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260123} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8b210e-ceef-5d7e-a20b-9a79b427549e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260260} {:answer/id #uuid "be97a3e0-af5b-55f6-8a9d-668875533062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261556} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa02f2a-4b5c-5f74-9d43-bc4fc65096f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261568} {:answer/id #uuid "cacc2a97-a4f6-538c-9ceb-91985ed7b814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa02f2a-4b5c-5f74-9d43-bc4fc65096f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d22bdec-d0fd-5742-b103-7ec2dae7bac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d93ec47e-8065-5376-b8dd-7b5786ea175a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127324" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072b4d5b-a518-50b1-9fd5-372915316f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424444} {:answer/id #uuid "231a4e7e-143a-55ee-96ec-349dc902c4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424449} {:answer/id #uuid "a888cc31-d112-55bf-8f2c-cc635b42c57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424481} {:answer/id #uuid "e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424493} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ef2656-ec3b-5442-90ba-703850ba3398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e38494e3-564d-5089-a5ec-aa68869b4e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424493} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e0080e-3bc6-5b4f-ad81-a8b266b3aa68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f963f5f-3418-5f15-a044-1af0d45a6d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127305" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e0bf7b88eadd199edc1997410b7ddbaa74b204e7d0f8afef7c9b17c28d8f8dc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[{x}^{2}-4yx]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({1}^{2}-4y(1))-((-1)^{2}-4y(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-8y\\,dy \\\\  & =-4{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-4(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afc0e4b-7ec3-5e04-a320-b09bbb7450d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424446} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebdbb3a-9cd8-57c7-9768-3437a54bb5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424460} {:answer/id #uuid "783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424467} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb053a-ae1f-53f2-9912-d9d9157552fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424476} {:answer/id #uuid "db270bba-95df-51f5-b9d1-ccab96038132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424467} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f3b20c-fcac-5124-a093-6cfd6cef919f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5645d046-8b12-5960-a3e0-e0429413dd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-127337" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\,{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1))-(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036be383-69ad-5c54-97f4-245cc64ebdc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424443} {:answer/id #uuid "84832c07-1cf7-5890-95f5-93379bbcf9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424470} {:answer/id #uuid "a513c7fb-0fe0-56ce-955a-00f68e2e1226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424479} {:answer/id #uuid "ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424482} {:answer/id #uuid "eb98dc31-4f54-5896-a0d6-e621b6d1e1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac21a517-9ce2-51e1-a210-d1342e658153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424482} :answer-field/id #uuid "501779b1-729b-5f0b-8f00-ddfd19deba04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "621adbef-22f1-5cf5-b77b-1bf6fb7f602e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127326" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7400787e09600d370fa5c554e616fad6ba3a126a09725cfb236502f81a7e70e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{e}^{y}-4xy$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4y-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4xy-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(-4(1)^{2}y-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-4y-6{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[-2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3})-(-2(-1)^{2}-2(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60eea807-02ce-5996-b952-62ec842109d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424461} {:answer/id #uuid "799a4193-8f0b-5d08-a4bf-2ba532ac8bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424468} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd7d3e5-183d-50b2-bbd9-75b14c583ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424469} {:answer/id #uuid "974a14ee-cedf-5300-8d94-d8f4a66e99c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424475} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424502} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4aac36-d301-5889-827c-4b73f4384b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2a7e7d-9054-51ec-bb29-4e186ac65a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-127307" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\,{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb0033d2b275442ee92cc0b805c71102641c670ea1b0edba97d31a9262795c83.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,x\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2{y}^{4}+2{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}+2(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}]}
